feat(reconciler): promote the ready-demand scan to the sweep's routed-work view

Q2 resolved yes-with-promotion (WC council, ga-f7v2ft.117). The per-store
ReadyLive read stops being a fingerprint hash input and becomes the detector
sweep's DECLARED routed-work view: one bounded read per store per patrol,
serving two consumers off one read - the demand snapshot's invalidation and
(next commit) the pool-allocation enqueue by exact
(workID, poolTarget, sourceStore) key.

Deleted together per the WE ledger, each verified to have no remaining live
caller: readyDemandSnapshotFingerprint, writeReadyDemandFingerprintBead,
flooredReadyDemandSnapshotFingerprint, readyDemandFingerprintFloor, the
runtimeDemandSnapshot.readyDemandFingerprint field, and
requestReadyRoutedWorkLegacyFallback with its readyRoutedWorkPokePending flag.

Invalidation moves from a fingerprint carried on the snapshot to a change edge
detected at the read and consumed exactly once by the next refresh; the first
observation raises no edge, because a runtime with no snapshot rebuilds for
that reason alone. The view's hash input is the declared projection
(store, id, resolved pool target, status, type, kind, contract, assigned), not
the raw bead: UpdatedAt is deliberately gone, since it rebuilt desired state on
every touch for a change no demand decision could see.

Pool-allocation overflow and unhandled/failed allocations are now census-owed
re-detection: recordRoutedWorkPoolAllocationOverflow traces the dropped key and
returns. The legacy poke is retired at all four sites, so a routed key never
crosses back to the legacy pool builder.

WD.16's cadence-floor tests move onto the promoted read (readyRoutedWorkViewFloor
/ readyRoutedWorkViewAt), keeping the same cadence contract.

